    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (80.16) stands as our premier play, benefiting from a highly favorable matchup against Klassen and his 5.56 xERA. This is the optimal environment for **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** to capitalize on, especially with the boost from his #3 lineup placement. Similarly, **Lawrence Butler** (73.46) demands attention as a high-upside start; he is buoyed by a 13% platoon edge and a favorable park factor, making him a major offensive anchor. While **Zack Gelof** (61.31) and **JJ Bleday** (63.34) carry lower base projections, both are elevated by situational tailwinds\u2014**Zack Gelof** benefits from a slow opposing pitcher delivery that boosts his stolen base expectancy, and **JJ Bleday** receives a substantial 14% platoon advantage against a weak opposing arm.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved **Gavin Sheets**, who narrowly missed the starting cut with a score of 39.23. The 40.0 floor for daily efficiency was the primary barrier; despite a 7% platoon boost, the combination of a tough matchup against Ashcraft and a suboptimal #7 spot in the order kept **Gavin Sheets** on the bench to preserve our limited game caps. We are also proceeding with caution regarding **Justin Crawford** (43.52) and **Cal Raleigh** (41.82). While **Justin Crawford** remains in the lineup to chase stolen base upside, his #9 spot acts as a significant drag on his score. **Cal Raleigh** is facing a stiff test against Wheeler; the -19% SP skill penalty significantly dampens his output, placing him squarely in the \"marginal\" tier. We will monitor weather reports for all venues, but for now, the primary risk remains the high-caliber arms of Gilbert and Wheeler facing our hitters like **Bryce Harper** (52.32) and **Luis Arraez** (45.54), who must contend with elite-tier pitching talent to maintain their offensive utility.",
